index 7afcb7c..c3c30ba 100644
--- a/docs/exception-handling.rst
+++ b/docs/exception-handling.rst
@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@
 Exception Handling
 ##################
 
-Schedule doesn't catch exceptions that happen during job execution. Therefore any exceptions thrown during job execution will bubble up and interrupt schedule's run_xyz function.
+Schedule doesn't swallow exceptions that happen during job execution. A job that raises is still rescheduled, so it will not re-run on every ``run_pending()`` call.
+
+When ``run_pending()`` is called, every due job runs even if an earlier one fails. After all of them have run, the first exception is re-raised. Any further exceptions from the same call are logged to the ``schedule`` logger. ``run_all()`` still stops at the first exception.
 
 If you want to guard against exceptions you can wrap your job function
 in a decorator like this:
